0:{"rsc":["$","$1","c",{"children":[[["$","$L2","5.11",{"topic":{"id":"5.11","title":"Machine Learning Applications in Cyber Forensics","minutes":15,"summary":"Connect learning methods to forensic tasks while keeping validation, explainability, and evidential limitations visible.","sections":[{"title":"Classification, clustering, and anomaly detection","body":["Classification can support malware-family labeling, message filtering, or file categorization. Clustering can group related artifacts without predefined labels. Anomaly detection can highlight unusual processes or network behavior. None of these methods automatically establishes who performed an action or whether the behavior satisfies a criminal offence.","Decision trees and random forests can provide interpretable feature-based decisions; support vector machines and neural networks may capture different relationships. Clustering results depend on distance measures and feature scaling. An unusual event can be a new legitimate business process. Choose a method that fits the data and question, then validate it appropriately."]},{"title":"Evaluation and forensic validity","body":["Separate training, validation, and test data. Prevent duplicates and related records from leaking across splits. Evaluate with metrics suited to prevalence and harm, and examine errors by relevant subgroup or scenario. A benchmark result from a public dataset may not transfer to a different device, application version, or network.","Document preprocessing, feature extraction, model parameters, random seeds where relevant, and software versions. Preserve the original evidence and the derived feature table. Where a proprietary model cannot be fully inspected, state that limitation and seek independent performance testing and corroboration."]},{"title":"Explainability and courtroom communication","body":["Feature importance, local explanations, and example-based reasoning can help an examiner understand a model. They do not prove causation or guarantee that the model used a legally relevant reason. A classifier may rely on an incidental artifact correlated with the training labels rather than the underlying malicious behavior.","Present the model as one component of the examination. Explain what it predicted, how it was tested, where it failed, and what independent evidence supports the finding. Do not translate a score into a claim about the probability of guilt. The court needs an accountable explanation of the evidence, not a leaderboard result."]}],"practice":{"title":"Review a model-validation claim","scenario":"A paper reports 99% accuracy on a malware dataset with many duplicate samples.","steps":["Identify possible leakage and prevalence problems.","Request precision, recall, confusion matrix, and split methodology.","Test whether the dataset matches the intended environment.","Write a limitation suitable for a forensic report."],"debrief":"A high headline metric is not enough. Validity depends on data separation, representativeness, error analysis, and the intended use."},"terms":[],"lawIds":["bsa","dpdp"],"sources":["owasp-ml","bsa","enisa"],"labId":"ai","unit":5,"objectives":["Explain classification, clustering, and anomaly detection and apply it to an investigation.","Explain evaluation and forensic validity and apply it to an investigation.","Explain explainability and courtroom communication and apply it to an investigation."],"question":{"id":"q-5.11","prompt":"Why can duplicate samples inflate a model’s test performance?","options":["They make the model impossible to run.","Near-identical examples may appear in both training and test sets.","They guarantee perfect real-world performance.","They eliminate the need for labels."],"answer":1,"explanation":"Leakage between splits can measure memorization or similarity rather than genuine generalization.","unit":5}}}]],["$L3"],"$L4"]}],"isPartial":false,"staleTime":300,"varyParams":null,"buildId":"mMiCr23A_-Cpk0hZkfT7X"}
